Suppose that $t=r^{\ell}$, where $r$ is a prime number, $\ell \geq 1$ and $d<r^\ell-r^{\ell-1}$.
Then $Q_{r^\ell}(x)$ is reducible and we proceed as in case (c) to prove (d).

Finally, suppose that $t=r^{\ell}$, where $r$ is a prime number, $\ell \geq 1$ and $d=r^\ell-r^{\ell-1}$.
Thus ${\ord}_{r^v}(2^m)=r^{v}-r^{v-1}$, for all $1\leq v \leq \ell$ and
all $Q_{r^v}(x)$ are irreducible.
In this case, a monic factor of degree $k$ of $x^{n}-1$ is of the form 
$$
g_{a}(x)=(x+1)^{a_0}Q_r(x)^{a_1}\cdots Q_{r^{\ell}}(x)^{a_{\ell}},
$$ 
with $a_0+a_1(r-1)+\dots + a_{\ell}(r^{\ell}-r^{\ell-1})=2^sr^{\ell}$. Define $b_j=a_j-2^s$. Observe that $g_a(x)$ satisfies condition (A) if and only if there exists a nonzero $(\ell+1)$-tuple of integers $(b_0,\dots,b_{\ell})$ satisfying
%$(b_0,\dots,b_{\ell})\neq (0,\dots,0)$, 
$|b_i|\leq 2^s$, for $0\leq i\leq \ell$, $b_0\neq -2^s$ and $$b_0+b_1(r-1)+\dots+b_{\ell}(r^{\ell}-r^{\ell-1})=0.$$
%$-(2^s-1)\leq b_0\leq 2^s$, $|b_i|\leq 2^s$, for $1\leq i\leq \ell$ and $$b_0+b_1(r-1)+\dots+b_{\ell}(r^{\ell}-r^{\ell-%1})=0.$$
Let $(b_0,\dots,b_{\ell})$ be such an $(\ell+1)$-tuple and
$j=\max\{\,i\, |\, b_i\neq 0\}$. Then the last equation becomes 
$$b_0+b_1(r-1)+\dots+b_{j}(r^{j}-r^{j-1})=0.$$
If $r>2^{s}+1$, then 
\begin{eqnarray*}
|b_0+b_1(r-1)+\dots+b_{j-1}(r^{j-1}-r^{j-2})| &\leq & 2^s(1+(r-1)+\dots+(r^{j-1}-r^{j-2}))\\

